\details{
The simulator uses the branching representation of an ETAS process. First,
background or immigrant events are generated from a Poisson process. The
expected number of immigrants is

\deqn{\mu (t_{\max} - t_{\min}),}

while their spatial density is proportional to
\eqn{\exp\{\eta_{\mathrm{bg}}(x,y)\}} and is numerically normalised over
the rectangular observation window. The background predictor may be
constant, linear in built-in or user-generated features, or supplied as a
custom function.

Conditional on its magnitude and covariates, every event may produce direct
offspring. Magnitudes are generated above \code{m0} according to a
Gutenberg--Richter distribution with parameter \code{b}. The expected number of
direct offspring is controlled by \code{k0} and by the triggering linear
predictor, which by default depends on relative magnitude. Offspring times
follow a modified Omori law governed by \code{c} and \code{p}, whereas offspring
locations follow the isotropic spatial triggering kernel governed by \code{d}
and \code{q}. The branching construction is iterated generation by generation
until no new offspring are produced or the optional explosion guard is
reached.

Complete genealogy is retained. \code{event_id} identifies each simulated event,
\code{father_id} is zero for an immigrant and otherwise identifies its direct
parent, and \code{lgen} records the branching generation. Thus \code{lgen = 0}
identifies background events, \code{lgen = 1} their direct offspring, and so on.

When \code{longlat.to.km = TRUE}, the original longitude and latitude coordinates
are converted to the planar working coordinates used by the ETAS triggering
calculation. Both representations are retained in the returned pattern and
catalog. Similarly, \code{sectoday} controls the interpretation of raw simulated
times when the package-native ETAS catalog is constructed.

The explosion guard is a numerical safety mechanism rather than part of the
stochastic model. If the cumulative number of events exceeds \code{max_events},
branching is stopped and the returned object's \code{exploded} component is set
to \code{TRUE}.
